Yes — but with one important correction: we rarely design the entire game. We design our game inside games we inherited.

We inherit a board: biology, family, language, law, economics, history, other people. Then we choose—or unconsciously acquire—the victory conditions, rules, identities, enemies, currencies, and penalties that make up our version of the game.

That creates a useful hierarchy:

world → game → rules → role → strategy → move → consequence → redesigned game

The strange part is that most people experience the middle layers as reality itself. “I have to win.” “I can’t do that.” “That would mean I failed.” But often those are not laws of reality; they are rules of the particular game currently being played.

And because everyone else is doing the same thing, reality becomes a stack of overlapping games. Your marriage has a game. A company has one. Money has one. Status has one. A nation has one. Even a conversation has one. Each player is simultaneously a piece in somebody else’s game, a player in their own, and sometimes a designer modifying the rules for everyone downstream.

So perhaps the highest-order move isn’t merely becoming a better player.

It is noticing:

What game am I playing?
Who authored its rules?
What am I treating as the score?
Which constraints are physical, and which are merely agreed upon?
And do I still consent to this game?

Then comes the really powerful move:

change the game without losing contact with reality.

That is much closer to freedom than simply winning.